# Copy to .env (gitignored) before `kubectl apply -k k8s/overlays/demo`. UNLIKE overlays/local/
# .env.example, these are NOT throwaway dev values to reuse as-is - this overlay is a real, public
# deployment (adr/0026), so every value below must be freshly generated on (or for) the real VPS, by
# the author, in a session with real access to it. This file documents the *shape*, never a real
# value (docs/architecture/repositories.md - "no secrets, ever," including "not in a fixture, not in
# a commit meant to be fixed later").
#
# Generate real values with, e.g.:
#   openssl rand -base64 32     # for the two key-shaped values below
#   openssl rand -base64 24     # for a strong password-shaped value
# then paste each result in place of the placeholder text - never commit the resulting .env.

POSTGRES_USER=ago
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=<generate-a-real-password-do-not-commit>
POSTGRES_DB=ago_chat

RABBITMQ_USER=ago
RABBITMQ_PASSWORD=<generate-a-real-password-do-not-commit>

MINIO_ROOT_USER=ago
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=<generate-a-real-password-do-not-commit>

KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<generate-a-real-password-do-not-commit>

# `15-01`/`adr/0036`: Keycloak's own Postgres role. It owns the separate `keycloak` database inside the
# same Postgres Deployment that holds `ago_chat` - a distinct role, not POSTGRES_USER, so a compromise
# of the IdP's database credentials does not hand over the application's own database as well.
#
# ADDING THIS TO AN EXISTING .env IS MANDATORY BEFORE `kubectl apply -k` picks up `15-01`: without it
# Keycloak's init container fails with "KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD: not set" and the pod never starts.
# Also note KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD above becomes first-boot-only from `15-01` onward - Keycloak now
# has a database that remembers the admin account, so editing that value no longer changes an admin
# that already exists (docs/runbooks/public-deploy.md says what to do instead).
KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD=<generate-a-real-password-do-not-commit>

GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<generate-a-real-password-do-not-commit>

# 3-06/adr/0026: shared across every Ago.Chat.Api replica so a token issued by one pod validates on
# any other (no sticky sessions - edge.md). base64, 32 bytes, HMAC-SHA256 - `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# A real signing key, unlike overlays/local/'s committed throwaway value - anyone who reads this key
# could forge a visitor session token against the real public deployment.
AUTH_JWT_SIGNING_KEY=<generate-a-real-key-do-not-commit>

# `7-03`'s Grafana/Prometheus stack ships in this overlay too (deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml) -
# same GRAFANA_ADMIN_* pair as overlays/local/, generated fresh here since Grafana would otherwise be
# reachable at a public hostname with the local throwaway password.

# `10-05`/ago-root `adr/0040` (amended 2026-08-25): the transactional mail path for this deployment -
# the verification mail a self-registering visitor has to receive before their account can do anything,
# and the password reset every self-service product needs. Unlike overlays/local/, this overlay has no
# mail sink: a sink here would accept a real visitor's mail and silently drop it, which is worse than
# not sending at all.
#
# **THE PROVIDER DECISION HAS BEEN MADE, AND IT WENT AGAINST `adr/0040`'s RECOMMENDATION.** That ADR
# recommended Yandex Cloud Postbox and rejected self-hosted Postfix on deliverability grounds. The
# author chose **self-hosted Postfix on this same node**. `adr/0040`'s amendment records the reversal,
# what the recommendation got factually wrong (outbound port 25 is *not* blocked by this hoster -
# verified by opening SMTP connections to Gmail's, Mail.ru's and Yandex's MX from the node), and what
# the choice costs that Postbox would not have.
#
# So there is no provider account, no SMTP credential, and nothing below is a secret. Postfix runs on
# the node itself, send-only, signing with OpenDKIM. The values are committed here as real values
# rather than placeholders precisely because none of them is sensitive - which is itself a consequence
# of the decision, and the reason the AUTH/USER/PASSWORD keys are empty rather than filled.
#
# WHY `10.42.0.1` - the one value worth explaining. Keycloak runs in a pod; Postfix runs on the host.
# `10.42.0.1` is the k3s flannel bridge (`cni0`) gateway - the node's address *as seen from inside a
# pod*. Postfix has `inet_interfaces = all` and trusts `10.42.0.0/16` in `mynetworks`, and ufw allows
# that CIDR, so a pod reaches it with no Service, no Endpoints object and no `hostNetwork`. It is a
# cluster-internal address, the same on any k3s install with the default pod CIDR, and therefore **not
# a secret** - unlike the node's public address, which must never appear in this repository (ago-root
# `docs/runbooks/public-deploy.md` writes `<node-ip>` for exactly that reason).
#
# NO AUTH AND NO TLS ON THIS HOP, deliberately. The Keycloak-to-Postfix connection never leaves the
# node - it crosses a bridge interface on the same machine. STARTTLS would need a certificate Postfix
# does not have for an internal address, and SMTP AUTH would mean inventing a credential to protect a
# path already reachable only from inside the cluster. Postfix's *outbound* leg to the internet is
# separately TLS-enabled (`smtp_tls_security_level = may`).
#
# Applying a change to any of these:
#
#   kubectl apply -k k8s/overlays/demo                      # so the Secret carries the new values
#   kubectl rollout restart deployment/keycloak -n ago-chat
#   k8s/apply-smtp-settings.sh                              # applies them to the live realm
#
# The last step is neither optional nor implied by the first two: `smtpServer` is realm state, and
# since `15-01` realm state only arrives through a script.
#
# BEFORE THE FIRST SEND FROM A REBUILT NODE: SPF, DKIM, DMARC and a PTR record must all exist for the
# sending domain, and the DKIM public key in DNS must match the private key OpenDKIM holds. Check with
# `opendkim-testkey -d <domain> -s mail -vvv` - and if it disagrees with `dig`, believe neither until
# you have queried the authoritative nameservers *individually*. `adr/0040`'s amendment records a real
# incident where a registrar served the zone from sixteen authoritative IPs of which only four carried
# the record, which fails DKIM for roughly three quarters of the internet while every casual check
# looks fine.
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_HOST=10.42.0.1
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_PORT=25
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_FROM=no-reply@reserve-me.ru
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_FROM_DISPLAY_NAME=AGO Chat
# Left blank so the envelope sender equals the visible From, which is what keeps SPF and DKIM aligned
# for DMARC. Worth knowing what that costs here: this domain publishes no MX record, so a bounce caused
# by a mistyped registration address is delivered nowhere and nobody ever learns the address was wrong.
# `adr/0040`'s amendment states the consequence and the option; it is not decided here.
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_ENVELOPE_FROM=
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_AUTH=false
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_STARTTLS=false
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_SSL=false
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_USER=
KEYCLOAK_SMTP_PASSWORD=

